The 0/1 Loss
A simple loss function could be the 0/1 loss, which is trivial in some sense. The 0/1 loss simply counts the number of misclassifications.

The 0/1 loss function for a single example is defined as: \[L_{0/1}(y, \hat{y}) = \begin{cases}
    0 & \text{if } y = \hat{y} \\
    1 & \text{if } y \neq \hat{y}
\end{cases}\] where \(y\) is the true label and \(\hat{y}\) is the predicted label. The total 0/1 loss over a dataset is the number of misclassifications.

Representing Vectors Using a Basis
We know that a basis for a vector space provides a way to represent any vector in that space as a linear combination of basis vectors. Let \(\{b_1, b_2, \ldots, b_n\}\) be a basis for a vector space \(\mathcal{V}\). Then, any vectors \(x, y \in \mathcal{V}\) can be represented as: \[\label{eq:vector_representation_x}
x = \sum_{i=1}^{n} \psi_i b_i\] \[\label{eq:vector_representation_y}
y = \sum_{j=1}^{n} \lambda_j b_j\] where \(\psi_i\) and \(\lambda_j\) are scalar coefficients.
